--TEST--
A `final` class is not extended and a `final` method is not redeclared, while an ordinary override beside it still compiles
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `final` is the author's statement that a declaration is not specialized
// further. Both halves are refused where the offending declaration is
// written, which is the same place PHP refuses them.
final class Sealed {
    public function name(): string { return "sealed"; }
}

class Widened extends Sealed {}

class Base {
    public final function id(): uint { return 1; }

    // Not `final`, and the override of it below is legal — the refusal is
    // per member and not a property of having a `final` sibling.
    public function label(): string { return "base"; }
}

class Sub extends Base {
    public function id(): uint { return 2; }

    public function label(): string { return "sub"; }
}
// covers: lang:classes/inheritance
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0783]: `Sealed` is `final`, so no class extends it
%A
error[E0784]: `Base::id` is `final`, so no subclass redeclares it
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
